Three out of four U.S. adults say they have no interest in buying a foldable iPhone, according to a new survey from <a href="https://www.cnet.com/tech/mobile/foldable-phone-survey/"><em>CNET</em></a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/03/Foldable-iPhone-2023-Feature-Iridescent-1.jpg" alt="" width="2250" height="1266" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-995555" /><br/>
<em>CNET</em>'s survey was conducted online by YouGov between August 13 and 17, polling 2,605 U.S. adults. It found that 75% are not interested in an Apple foldable device, and those who are would not pay anywhere near the rumored &#36;2,000+ price.<br/>
<br/>
Interest is low across every generation surveyed. Boomers (1946–1964) and Gen Xers (1965–1980) are the least interested, at 83% and 77% respectively, while 69% of Gen Z (1997–2012) and 68% of millennials (1981–1996) also said they would pass on a foldable iPhone. <em>CNET</em> noted that a quarter of respondents still expressing interest in the device is a notable proportion for what remains a niche product category.<br/>
<br/>
On average, U.S. adults said they would spend just &#36;781 on a foldable iPhone, about &#36;48 less than the &#36;799 starting price of the standard <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17/">iPhone 17</a>. Gen Z was willing to spend the most, an average of &#36;919, followed by millennials at &#36;859, Gen X at &#36;690, and Boomers at &#36;572. That mismatch between expected pricing and what buyers actually want to pay highlights just how far a &#36;2,000+ device will sit outside most people's budgets.<br/>
<br/>
Moreover, <em>CNET</em> found that 41% of U.S. adults are rethinking their upgrade plans because of anticipated price increases. Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/25/apple-explains-why-it-raised-prices/">raised prices</a> on most of its devices in June, citing the global memory shortage, though iPhone, Apple Watch, and AirPods prices were untouched at the time. The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a> lineup is <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/24/apple-preparing-to-increase-iphone-prices/">expected to cost</a> more than its predecessor when it launches for the same reasons.<br/>
<br/>
5% of respondents said they are considering buying an older or cheaper model, 4% are considering switching brands, and 4% are looking at refurbished or used phones. Another 9% said they will only upgrade if a trade-in or carrier promotion covers the cost. 22% of U.S. adults said they have no plans to upgrade anytime soon regardless of what Apple releases, ranging from 16% of millennials to 27% of Boomers.<div class="linkback">Related Roundup: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-fold/">iPhone Fold</a></div><div class="linkback">Tags: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/cnet-com/">CNET</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/foldable-iphone/">Foldable iPhone</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/26/most-arent-interested-in-a-foldable-iphone/">75% of US Adults Aren&#039;t Interested in a Foldable iPhone</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/75-of-us-adults-arent-interested-in-a-foldable-iphone.2488011/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>